{"step_index":507,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-25T12:06:42Z","input_tokens":3439,"cache_read_tokens":38194,"output_tokens":2496,"content":"### Implementation Complete: Phase 2 & Phase 3 Visualizations\n\nBoth **Phase 2 (Animate Variables & Calculations)** and **Phase 3 (Visualize Conditions & Loops)** have been integrated into the Code Flow Visualizer without rebuilding from scratch, without external AI APIs, and while preserving the real deterministic execution engine and user code.\n\n---\n\n### Files Modified\n\n| File | Changes Made |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [index.html](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/index.html) | Added the **`✨ Animations: ON / OFF`** toggle in the top bar; added dedicated visual containers in Area 2: `#loopReturnBanner`, `#visualLoopStrip`, `#visualConditionGate`, `#visualCalcStage`, and `#negativeResetVisual`. |\n| [styles.css](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/styles.css) | Defined styles and keyframe animations (`popIn`, `slideRightToBox`, `highlightPulse`, `arrowPulse`, `shake`) for variable boxes, calculation combining stages, condition gates, loop element tracks, return banners, and a zero-latency `body.no-animations` override. |\n| [app.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/app.js) | Implemented 6 reactive visual renderers bound to execution events: `renderVisualVariableBoxes()`, `renderVisualCalcStage()`, `renderNegativeResetVisual()`, `renderVisualConditionGate()`, `renderVisualLoopStrip()`, and `renderLoopReturnBanner()`, plus branch indicator badges in the code viewer. |\n| [explainer.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/explainer.js) | Verified substep metadata generation, ensuring real execution values (`operationDetail`, `beforeVars`, `condition`, `loop`) flow directly to the visual layer. |\n\n---\n\n### Feature Breakdown\n\n#### Phase 2 — Animate Variables and Calculations\n1. **Labeled Variable Boxes (`.var-box-card`)**:\n   - Variables (`c`, `ans`, `i`) render as labeled visual containers showing variable names, current runtime values, and a badge indicating previous values (e.g. `(was -2)`).\n2. **Number Moving into a Variable (`.assign-animation-row`)**:\n   - When a variable is assigned or updated, the computed number appears in a source chip (`.anim-source-val`) and moves via an animated directional arrow (`──► moves into ──►`) into the variable's box.\n3. **Step-by-Step Arithmetic Calculation (`#visualCalcStage`)**:\n   - On compound operations such as `c += nums[i]`, operands are displayed in individual calculation chips (e.g., `c = 0` and `nums[1] = 1`), combined across the `+` operator, and animated into the new sum before binding into `c`.\n4. **Value Transition Animation**:\n   - Updated variables flash with an emerald pulse (`highlightPulse`) and scale in (`popIn`), distinguishing newly created variables from modified ones.\n5. **Negative Value Reset to Zero Visual (`#negativeResetVisual`)**:\n   - When `c < 0` triggers Kadane's reset, a visual alert appears showing the negative running sum crossed out and discarded (`-2` struck through), transitioning into a fresh `c = 0 (Fresh Start)` box with an explanation of why negative sums are discarded.\n6. **Short, Optional Animations**:\n   - All animations run in **0.35s – 0.6s**.\n   - Clicking **`✨ Animations: ON / OFF`** instantly disables all transitions via `body.no-animations` for users who prefer static updates.\n\n---\n\n#### Phase 3 — Visualize Conditions and Loops\n1. **Comparisons Using Actual Values (`#visualConditionGate`)**:\n   - Condition evaluations (e.g., `if c >= ans:`) render as a visual comparison gate displaying the evaluated expressions and concrete values side-by-side (e.g., `c (-2)` vs `ans (-2)`).\n2. **Clear True / False Decision Badge**:\n   - Prominently displays a color-coded badge: **`✓ TRUE`** (green) or **`✗ FALSE`** (rose) based on the evaluated condition.\n3. **Branch Execution Highlighting**:\n   - The decision gate highlights the branch that executes (`✓ EXECUTES`) and dims the bypassed branch (`SKIPPED`).\n   - Line numbers in the code editor (Area 1) also display inline outcome badges: `[✓ executes]` or `[✗ skipped]`.\n4. **Loop Index and Element Tracker (`#visualLoopStrip`)**:\n   - For `for i in range(len(nums)):`, a horizontal array strip displays all elements in `nums`.\n   - An active pointer (`▲ pointer`) highlights the current index `i` (e.g., `Pass 3/9 • i = 2 • nums[2] = -3`), scrolling horizontally as the loop advances.\n5. **Visual Return to Loop Header (`#loopReturnBanner`)**:\n   - When an iteration completes and execution loops back to line 18, a banner appears with an indicator:\n     `↺ Looping back to Line 18: Pass 1 finished! Advancing loop variable i = 1 (next list element: 1)`.\n\n---\n\n### Verification\n\n- **Real Engine Integrity**: All variables, conditions, and array lookups derive from actual Python runtime evaluation in [engine.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/engine.js). No values are fabricated.\n- **Maximum Subarray Example**: Verified with `nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]`. The final computed answer correctly completes at **`ans = 6`**.\n- **Navigation**: Previous Step, Next Step, F7 Sub-step, Restart, and Auto Play maintain synchrony between code lines, visual boxes, loop pointers, and memory stack/heap views.","thinking":"Confirmed the implementation covers displaying variables as labeled boxes with current values, leveraging the designated rendering functions and corresponding class names. Verified that animation of values assigned to variables is also addressed through the appropriate rendering function and class names.\n\n\nVisualizations are structured to clarify calculations with step-by-step arithmetic displayed using operators and operand values, culminating in a result animation. Variable updates are highlighted with an indicator showing the previous value and a pulse animation. Negative value resets are visualized to show the discard and fresh zero reset, along with a brief explanation. Conditionals and loops are addressed by showing values in comparative statements, resulting in a clear \"True\" or \"False\" outcome, and highlighting the executed branch.\n\n\nVisual cues for branching logic involve differentiating active and skipped branches using distinct class names and inline markers. Loop visualization focuses on `renderVisualLoopStrip` for showing the current index, list element, an active pointer, and an information pill. A loop return banner, `renderLoopReturnBanner`, will visually indicate when the loop restarts with the updated index variable.\n\n\n"}
